---
  import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
  import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
  import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
  import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Architecture — SAARTHI-STUDIO"
  description="How SAARTHI-STUDIO is put together: the local service, the UI shell, the agent loop, and the platform clients."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Architecture"
    title="One local service, several surfaces"
    description="A single Rust service owns your conversations, files, agents, and model traffic. The UI is the same whether it runs in the native window or a browser."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>The runtime</h2>
      <p>
        One Rust binary (crate and binary <code>SAARTHI-STUDIO</code>) serves the
        loopback UI and API at <code>127.0.0.1:8765</code>. The
        <code>gui</code> feature adds a native window over Tauri 2. The
        frontend is hand-written static HTML/CSS/JS served from the Rust
        server, with no build step and no package manager in the app.
      </p>
      <p>
        SQLite persists conversations, runs, steps, keys, and usage at
        <code>~/.local/share/SAARTHI-STUDIO/studio.db</code>; configuration
        lives at <code>~/.config/SAARTHI-STUDIO/config.json</code>. These paths
        are fixed by design.
      </p>

      <h2>The agent loop</h2>
      <p>
        Agents are bounded run loops: a run owns steps, each step owns
        approvals, diffs, and checkpoints. The Runs view shows every step
        log, token usage, and outcome, and running agents can hand their
        worktree back to you for the final commit.
      </p>

      <h2>The platform clients</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Desktop.</strong> Native window (Tauri 2) or <code>--browser</code>; the same UI either way.</li>
        <li><strong>Android.</strong> A native companion client: direct router mode, or Desktop Host mode paired over a secured Host API (TLS + pairing token). Host-mode chat is non-streaming and therefore stays off the phone.</li>
        <li><strong>VS Code.</strong> A TypeScript extension with an embedded Node server implementing a subset of the desktop routes — no terminal panel, no router supervision, no PTY.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The HTTP surface</h2>
      <p>
        The main listeners are the loopback UI/API and the opt-in
        <code>/api/host/v1/*</code> Host API, which is a separate listener
        with a bearer token and TLS. The loopback contract is local-only; the
        Host API is a documented remote contract. API routes are documented
        in the
        <a href={SITE.readme} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">README</a>
        and in the engineering docs.
      </p>

      <div class="note-card">
        <Icon name="database" size={18} />
        <div>
          <strong>Source of truth.</strong>
          The diagrams and contracts on this page summarize
          <a href={SITE.architecture} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">docs/ARCHITECTURE.md</a>
          and
          <a href={SITE.readme} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">README.md</a>.
          Code is the final word.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
